  "id": "DrugBank_DB00928 - MONDO_18434",
  "verbalization": "Azacitidine is associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a series of indirect pathways, primarily via Idarubicin. The evidence suggests that Azacitidine may be repurposed for AML, as it is indicated for Inherited AML and Myeloid Leukemia, both of which are linked to Idarubicin. Idarubicin, in turn, is indicated for AML with a specific chromosomal translocation, T(9;11)(p22;q23). This implies that Azacitidine could potentially influence the same biological processes that Idarubicin targets, thereby supporting a repurposing relationship. The presence of overlapping indications and the shared mechanism of action, though not explicitly stated, suggests a possible functional link between Azacitidine and AML with the specified translocation. The evidence is consistent with a mechanism of action that involves the treatment of AML, though the exact molecular pathway remains unclear.",
